 these are run by an assistant  nurse  with clinic assistants who have only  five weeks training before tackling work  like malaria cases  they also run special clinics for  children under five  keeping a record of each child's health  and giving immunization  at these clinics they also supply a  daily food supplement  wheat mixed with chickpea to 4 000 young  children  the assistants often visit women in  their homes giving advice on nutrition  immunizations and family planning  a doctor isn't needed for any of these  jobs but he visits each sub-center twice  a week for serious cases  [Music]  health cannot be studied as something  separate in a community  it is linked to every aspect of  development to sanitation and clean  water supplies  and to food production  especially in countries where there is  heavy population pressure on the land  health care depends very directly upon  the people being able to make the most  productive use of all the land available  only in that way will large families be  able to enjoy enough nutritious food  so how broad should the functions of a  health worker be  there is no single answer it is  different between countries  and within a single country  panama is a country with 9 000 small  communities  scattered in the mountains and along  jungle rivers  each group of communities has to work  out its own needs  and how to tackle them  near cologne a village began with a  household survey  collecting facts on people of all ages  [Music]  they paid particular concern to the  growth and the state of health of the  younger children  members of the community health  committee across section of the  villagers  analyzed the statistics from the survey  then they held a meeting for the whole  community putting the facts before them  they heard people's concerns and  together they planned a community  program of action  one measure they decided on with  hospital officials and the medical  officer who's in charge of public health  in the province  was to choose and train a medical  assistant  the people elected their own medical  assistant marciana harado  someone they knew could do the job they  had just defined  she is trained at the district health  center and goes back there once a week  for further training  and to get medical supplies  she is also trained how to move around  the community encouraging people in many  efforts of self-improvement  she is mostly on her own and coping with  a wide range of jobs  that go beyond the normal boundaries of  mother and child health care  for example she advises on improvements  to sanitation  in three years 40 other assistants like  marciana  have been selected and given basic  training in cologne  and they are now working as part of the  provincial health team  in their villages they are the health  team and something more important  they have shown the community what it  can do once it starts to mobilize its  own resources  in a very different culture in northeast  thailand  the medical faculty of khan ken  university asked officials in 16  provinces  what was most wanted in health care back  came the answer from the villages  someone nearby to consult when we are  ill  so dr kawi and his staff began simple  training courses in 1975  for village health volunteers unlike  bangladesh and panama  these are part-time health workers who  earn their living by farming  training courses are short two or three  weeks at a time  but they attend several courses over a  period of two years  the university has found there is a need  for more appropriate teaching materials  for this new level of pupils  it makes do with some traditional  equipment but it does its utmost to give  realistic training  [Applause]  the series of short courses should equip  a village health worker with the skills  to treat  common illnesses mr michai a farmer who  lives 50 miles from khan kim  treats malaria and diarrhea gives advice  on nutrition and family planning  and tells villagers how to protect  themselves against parasites  he also knows when and how to refer  difficult cases  the villagers appreciate mr michai's  work  they built a small clinic for him  [Music]  in canada also some rural communities  have shown  they know best what they need in health  care  a group of indian leaders told health  officials the best way was a broad  educational approach  health they said wasn't just a segment  of somebody's life  it covered everything from how and where  they placed a house and found clean  water supplies  to their daily food needs  [Applause]  in 1964 the government of canada began  training community health  representatives at fort capel  saskatchewan  eugenie lavallee was one of the first  working in her own payapot indian  reserve  as she explains her job led her into  teaching many things  the minute i graduated from the fort  that was the first thing i did i had to  come and  talk to the chief and then the nurse and  i had a very good nurse i can't say that  much because she was always out there  if i had a meeting she was there and  that used to be a big  lift because at that time even i  belonged here i was still afraid you  know  i needed somebody there to give me  courage  i used to go by seasons accidents in the  winter  and you know in the spring it was x-rays  for tb and stuff like that and they  cleaned up  and then in july it used to be more or  less gardening and  so is in august it was judging of the  gardens and  but everything pertaining to health is  what you know  what i worked on on the long run i think  i  made it and i quit last year  not rich at all brooke but a lot of lot  of friends  she has retired now but is still very  much involved in community affairs  as an indian band counselor and member  of the school board  she has handed on her job on the health  team to violet payapot  violet works closely with the nurse who  visit your clinic  the nurse gives immunizations  violet keeps the health records of each  child  s